            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you regarding your patient, a 36-year-old male, admitted on 03/11/2007 with a two-day history of progressive right lower quadrant pain, nausea, and low-grade fever, clinically consistent with acute appendicitis. On admission, physical examination revealed rebound tenderness at McBurney's point. Blood tests confirmed elevated inflammatory markers, and abdominal ultrasound supported the diagnosis. The patient was resuscitated with intravenous fluids and commenced on analgesics and prophylactic antibiotics. Given the clinical picture, a laparoscopic appendectomy was performed on 04/11/2007 without intraoperative complications. The postoperative course was uneventful. Wound care was provided, and the patient was mobilised early with physiotherapy support. He was discharged home on 08/11/2007 in good general condition with outpatient follow-up instructions. Please do not hesitate to contact me should you require further information.</div>"
            },
